from common import *

B = bank(2, "unit-2-networks-of-exchange")
T_SR, T_MON, T_IO, T_TS, T_EF = ("silk-roads", "mongol-empire", "indian-ocean-trade", "trans-saharan-trade", "effects-of-connectivity")

B.mcq(T_MON, src("Report of a European envoy to the Mongol capital, 1250s", "“In the capital I found people of many nations: Christians, Muslims and worshippers of idols, each permitted to keep his own faith, so long as he paid tribute and obeyed the Khan's officers.”") + "\n\nThe passage best illustrates which characteristic of Mongol rule?",
      ch("Religious toleration combined with the use of diverse peoples in the service of the empire.", "Mongol rulers generally allowed subject peoples to practice their own religions while demanding loyalty and tribute, and they employed administrators and specialists from many cultures."),
      [ch("A requirement that all subjects convert to a single state religion.", "The passage shows a variety of faiths being practiced freely."),
       ch("Strict segregation of foreigners, who were barred from the Mongol capital.", "The envoy describes many nations living in the capital."),
       ch("The adoption of Christianity as the official faith of the Mongol court.", "The Mongol court did not adopt Christianity as an official faith, and the passage describes several religions side by side.")],
      "The passage shows a policy of toleration paired with demands for obedience and tribute.")

B.mcq(T_MON, "Which statement best explains the increase in long-distance trade across Eurasia in the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries?",
      ch("Mongol rule linked much of Eurasia under one authority, providing relative security and relay stations for merchants and envoys.", "The Mongol conquests unified overland routes, and measures such as the postal-relay system and safe-conduct passes helped make travel safer, an era often called the Pax Mongolica."),
      [ch("The collapse of every Eurasian state left the routes open and unregulated.", "Trade grew because large political authority provided security, not because authority disappeared."),
       ch("The Mongols banned long-distance trade in order to protect the economy of the steppe.", "The Mongols encouraged and taxed trade, and many Mongol rulers patronized merchants."),
       ch("Chinese emperors closed the borders to protect domestic silk production.", "Under the Yuan dynasty China was part of the Mongol system and was open to foreign merchants.")],
      "The security created by Mongol rule encouraged merchants and travelers.")

B.mcq(T_MON, "Which statement best explains why the Mongol Empire did not remain a single state after the thirteenth century?",
      ch("It was too vast to rule as one state, and it divided into regional khanates whose rulers adopted local religions and customs.", "Rivalries among Genghis Khan's descendants and the empire's size led to separate successor states such as the Yuan dynasty, the Ilkhanate, the Chagatai Khanate and the Golden Horde."),
      [ch("It was conquered in the 1250s by the Ottoman Empire.", "The Ottoman state did not yet exist as a major power in the 1250s, and it did not conquer the Mongols."),
       ch("Its rulers voluntarily abolished the empire to restore the previous kingdoms.", "The empire fragmented through succession disputes and distance, not voluntary abolition."),
       ch("It was destroyed by a united alliance of Song China and the Abbasid caliphate.", "The Mongols themselves ended the Song dynasty (1279), and they destroyed the Abbasid caliphate's capital in 1258.")],
      "Succession struggles and the size of the empire led to separate khanates.")

B.mcq(T_SR, src("Account by a notary of a Genoese trading port, 1347", "“Ships arriving from the Black Sea brought sailors already marked by the sickness. Within weeks the pestilence had spread from the harbor into the town, and neighbors fled from neighbors.”") + "\n\nThe spread of the disease described in the passage is best explained by which of the following?",
      ch("The movement of merchants and goods along interconnected land and sea trade routes.", "The Black Death traveled from Asia along trade routes, including those of the Mongol era, and reached Mediterranean ports by ship."),
      [ch("The return of Crusader armies from the Holy Land.", "The major crusades had ended long before the 1340s, and the plague came by trade routes, not armies."),
       ch("The Mongol conquest of Italy.", "The Mongols did not conquer Italy."),
       ch("The cutting off of all trade between Asia and Europe.", "The disease spread precisely because trade connections existed, not because they were cut.")],
      "The plague traveled the same routes that carried commerce between Asia and Europe.")

B.mcq(T_EF, "Which was a long-term consequence of the Black Death in western Europe?",
      ch("Labor shortages gave surviving peasants and workers more bargaining power and contributed to the decline of serfdom.", "With so many workers dead, laborers could demand higher wages and better terms, weakening the manorial system in much of western Europe."),
      [ch("A sudden increase in population that supplied lords with cheap labor.", "The plague reduced the population by a large share; labor became scarcer and more valuable."),
       ch("The immediate end of the Catholic Church's influence in Europe.", "The Church lost some prestige because it could not stop the plague, but it remained very influential."),
       ch("The strengthening of the manorial system and of peasants' obligations to their lords.", "The manorial system weakened; some lords tried to enforce old obligations, which led to revolts.")],
      "Fewer workers meant better terms for those who survived.")

B.mcq(T_IO, "Which combination best explains why the Indian Ocean was an active trading zone before 1450?",
      ch("Predictable monsoon winds, sailing technologies such as the lateen sail and the astrolabe, and a network of port cities linking East Africa, Arabia, India and Southeast Asia.", "Merchants could time voyages with the seasonal monsoons, use navigational tools, and rely on ports where goods were exchanged."),
      [ch("A single empire that controlled all the ports and fixed all prices.", "The Indian Ocean trade was open and diverse, with many competing states and city-states rather than one controlling empire."),
       ch("Steam-powered ships that could travel against the wind.", "Steamships were a nineteenth-century development."),
       ch("European navies that protected merchant ships beginning in 1200.", "European naval power came to the Indian Ocean only after about 1500.")],
      "Monsoons, navigation technology and port cities supported Indian Ocean trade.")

B.mcq(T_IO, "How did the voyages of the Ming dynasty admiral Zheng He (1405-1433) differ from the European voyages of exploration that began later in the fifteenth century?",
      ch("The Chinese expeditions were state-sponsored projects that displayed Ming power and extended the tribute system, and they did not establish permanent colonies.", "Zheng He's large fleets visited ports around the Indian Ocean to receive tribute and promote trade for the Ming court, and the voyages ended without colonial settlement."),
      [ch("They were financed by joint-stock companies seeking profit.", "Joint-stock companies were a European institution of the seventeenth century; Zheng He's voyages were sponsored by the Ming emperor."),
       ch("They established Chinese colonies along the East African coast.", "The voyages visited East Africa, but China did not establish colonies there."),
       ch("They were meant to find a sea route to Europe.", "The Ming voyages were directed toward Southeast Asia, India, Arabia and East Africa, not toward finding a route to Europe.")],
      "Zheng He's voyages were state-sponsored demonstrations of power, not colonizing ventures.")

B.mcq(T_IO, src("Account by a North African traveler of a visit to the East African coast, 1330s", "“The city is built of stone, and its people are devout Muslims. Gold comes to its markets from the south, and its merchants send it across the sea to Arabia and India in exchange for cloth and porcelain.”") + "\n\nThe passage best illustrates which of the following?",
      ch("The importance of Islam and Indian Ocean commerce to the prosperity of East African city-states.", "Swahili city-states such as Kilwa became wealthy by trading African gold and ivory for goods from across the Indian Ocean, and their culture was strongly influenced by Islam."),
      [ch("The dominance of Portuguese merchants over the East African coast.", "The Portuguese arrived in the region in the late fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries, after this passage."),
       ch("The isolation of the East African coast from long-distance trade.", "The passage describes active trade with Arabia and India."),
       ch("The reliance of East African cities on trans-Saharan caravans.", "East African ports traded across the Indian Ocean, not across the Sahara.")],
      "The passage shows an Islamic, trade-based city linked to the Indian Ocean.")

B.mcq(T_TS, "Which development best explains the growth of trans-Saharan trade in the first millennium CE?",
      ch("The use of domesticated camels, which were well suited to carrying goods across the desert in caravans.", "Camels can go long distances without water, allowing regular caravans to cross the Sahara."),
      [ch("The invention of the steam locomotive.", "Steam locomotives were a nineteenth-century invention."),
       ch("The construction of paved roads across the desert by the Mali Empire.", "The Sahara was crossed by caravan tracks, not paved roads."),
       ch("The use of horse-drawn wagons suited to loose sand.", "Wagons are poorly suited to deep sand; camels made desert crossings practical.")],
      "Camel caravans made regular crossings of the Sahara possible.")

B.mcq(T_TS, "Which statement best explains why the exchange of Saharan salt for West African gold was profitable for both sides?",
      ch("Salt was scarce in the forests and savannas to the south but available in the Sahara, while gold was plentiful in West Africa and highly valued in North Africa and the Mediterranean world.", "Each product was abundant at one end of the route and scarce at the other, so traders profited from carrying them in both directions."),
      [ch("Both commodities were used as the main currency in China.", "The trade centered on the Sahara, West Africa and the Mediterranean, not China."),
       ch("Salt was available everywhere in West Africa but gold was available only in North Africa.", "This reverses the actual distribution: gold came from West Africa and salt from Saharan deposits."),
       ch("European governments set fixed prices for salt and gold to encourage the trade.", "The trade was organized by African and North African merchants and states, and European governments did not set prices.")],
      "The trade worked because each commodity was scarce where the other was plentiful.")

B.mcq(T_SR, "Which statement best describes the effect of exchange along Eurasian trade routes on technology between 600 and 1450?",
      ch("Technologies such as paper, printing and gunpowder spread from China westward, eventually influencing Islamic and European societies.", "Chinese inventions traveled along the trade routes and were adopted and adapted in other regions."),
      [ch("Chinese rulers adopted the European printing press and spread it eastward.", "Printing began in China before the European printing press of the 1400s."),
       ch("Technologies did not travel, because trade routes carried only luxury goods.", "Along with goods, ideas and technologies were transmitted by merchants, travelers and conquerors."),
       ch("Only religions were exchanged, because merchants did not carry inventions.", "Inventions such as paper and gunpowder show that technological knowledge also spread.")],
      "Chinese inventions diffused westward along trade routes.")

B.mcq(T_EF, src("A merchant's handbook, Florence, about 1340", "“The road to Cathay is perfectly safe, by day and by night, according to merchants who have used it, provided that the merchant travels with a guide and a handful of servants.”") + "\n\nWhich condition most directly explains the claim made in the handbook?",
      ch("Mongol rule had brought relative political stability and security to the overland routes across Asia.", "The Mongol Empire policed trade routes and protected merchants, which is why European merchants could describe travel across Asia as safe."),
      [ch("The Roman Empire still maintained the roads across Central Asia.", "The Roman Empire had long since ended in the west and never controlled the routes across Central Asia."),
       ch("The collapse of long-distance trade made the roads nearly empty.", "The passage describes merchants using the route, and trade was active in this period."),
       ch("Chinese naval patrols protected overland caravans.", "Naval patrols could not protect overland routes, and the security came from Mongol authority.")],
      "The handbook's claim reflects the security created by Mongol rule.")

B.mcq(T_MON, "How did the Mongol rulers of the Yuan dynasty (1271-1368) govern China?",
      ch("They retained many Chinese administrative practices but gave important posts to foreigners and ranked Mongols highest in the social order.", "Yuan rulers used Chinese bureaucratic forms but relied on Mongols and foreigners, including Muslims and Europeans, in key positions and distrusted the Chinese elite."),
      [ch("They abolished all Chinese institutions and governed by tribal law alone.", "Yuan rulers kept much of the existing administration and many institutions."),
       ch("They withdrew to the steppe and allowed Chinese governors to rule independently.", "The Yuan court ruled China directly from its capital, Khanbaliq (Beijing)."),
       ch("They restored the civil service examinations immediately and recruited officials only by examination.", "The examinations were suspended for decades and restored only in 1315, and officials were not recruited only by examination.")],
      "The Yuan combined Chinese administration with Mongol and foreign dominance.")

B.mcq(T_EF, "Which statement best explains the economic role of Italian city-states such as Venice and Genoa in the thirteenth and fourteenth centuries?",
      ch("They served as intermediaries between Asian and Middle Eastern markets and European consumers, and their commercial wealth supported art and civic life.", "The Italian cities carried goods across the Mediterranean from the Islamic world and Black Sea ports, and the profits helped finance the culture of the Renaissance."),
      [ch("They controlled the overland routes across Central Asia.", "These routes were controlled by the Mongols and their successors; Italian merchants traveled them as visitors."),
       ch("They were governed by Mongol officials who collected tribute.", "Venice and Genoa were independent maritime republics."),
       ch("They had no merchant class and depended on subsistence agriculture.", "Their wealth and power rested on commerce, not subsistence farming.")],
      "Venice and Genoa profited as the middlemen of Mediterranean commerce.")

B.mcq(T_SR, "Which pairing correctly links a belief system with its main route of diffusion before 1450?",
      ch("Buddhism: from India to China and other parts of East Asia along the Silk Roads and by missionaries and merchants.", "Buddhist teachings traveled with monks and merchants along Central Asian trade routes, then adapted to Chinese culture."),
      [ch("Islam: to Southeast Asia mainly through conquest by Spanish armies.", "Spanish forces did not spread Islam; it reached Southeast Asia through Muslim merchants and Sufis."),
       ch("Christianity: to East Africa across the Sahara by Mongol armies.", "Mongol armies did not spread Christianity across the Sahara."),
       ch("Hinduism: to Europe by the Mongols along the Volga.", "Hinduism did not spread to Europe through the Mongols.")],
      "Buddhism spread from India to East Asia along trade routes.")

saq(B, T_SR,
    "Use the passage to answer parts A, B and C.\n\n" + src("Account by a notary of a Genoese trading port, 1347", "“Ships arriving from the Black Sea brought sailors already marked by the sickness. Within weeks the pestilence had spread from the harbor into the town, and neighbors fled from neighbors.”") + "\n\n**a)** Identify ONE way the passage shows that regions of Eurasia were connected by trade.\n\n**b)** Explain ONE historical development between 1200 and 1350 that made such connections possible.\n\n**c)** Explain ONE long-term consequence of the plague described in the passage for European society.",
    [(1, "Part A: Identifies that the disease arrived on ships from the Black Sea, showing that goods and people moved between regions"),
     (1, "Part B: Explains a development such as the Mongol conquests creating stable overland routes (the Pax Mongolica), the growth of Italian maritime trade, or improvements in shipping"),
     (1, "Part C: Explains a consequence such as labor shortages that improved workers' bargaining power and weakened serfdom, or a decline in the prestige of the Church")],
    "a) The passage describes ships arriving from the Black Sea carrying sailors who were already sick, which shows that people and goods regularly moved between regions and that disease traveled with them.\nb) The Mongol conquests united much of Eurasia, creating relatively secure overland routes (the Pax Mongolica) that connected China, Central Asia, the Black Sea and the Mediterranean, along with growing Italian commercial shipping.\nc) The Black Death killed a large share of Europe's population, creating labor shortages. Surviving peasants and workers could demand higher wages and more freedom, which weakened serfdom and manorial obligations in much of western Europe.")

leq(B, T_MON,
    "**Long Essay Question**\n\nEvaluate the extent to which the Mongol Empire transformed trade and cultural exchange across Eurasia in the period 1200-1450.",
    "A strong response takes a position, for example that the Mongols intensified and secured existing networks more than they created new ones. It places the topic in context (the existing Silk Roads, the growth of Muslim and Chinese commerce) and uses specific evidence such as the postal relay system and safe-conduct passes, the movement of Chinese inventions westward, the movement of European and Muslim merchants and envoys into Asia, and the spread of the Black Death. It analyzes change and continuity, weighing the benefits of Mongol security against the destruction caused by the conquests and the diseases that traveled the same routes.",
    "Thesis: Presents a defensible thesis that responds to the prompt and evaluates the extent of the Mongol transformation",
    "Contextualization: Describes broader context such as the existing Silk Road trade, the growth of commerce in the Islamic world and China, or the decline of earlier states",
    "Evidence: Provides at least two specific, accurate examples (such as the relay system, safe-conduct passes, the spread of paper, gunpowder and printing, European travelers to Asia, or the Black Death) and uses them to support the argument",
    "Analysis and reasoning: Uses continuity and change over time to structure the argument (1 point), and shows complexity, such as balancing the security of Mongol rule with the destruction of the conquests or the spread of disease (2nd point)")

B.save()
B.brief()
